00:04:27.910 --> 00:04:28.150
No.

138
00:04:28.470 --> 00:04:30.170
Here's an example of a fast fact.

139
00:04:30.310 --> 00:04:30.490
Okay.

140
00:04:31.090 --> 00:04:37.250
The show, The Office, is actually an adaptation of a British show of the same name, The Office.

141
00:04:37.430 --> 00:04:37.790
That's right.

142
00:04:37.870 --> 00:04:38.590
It was called The Office.

143
00:04:38.790 --> 00:04:40.550
It was written by Ricky Gervais and Stephen Merchant.

144
00:04:40.730 --> 00:04:41.770
It's fantastic.

145
00:04:42.050 --> 00:04:42.610
It is amazing.

146
00:04:42.650 --> 00:04:47.370
I was a huge fan of it before I even went to the audition for the American version of

147
00:04:47.370 --> 00:04:47.750
The Office.

148
00:04:47.930 --> 00:04:48.270
Same.

149
00:04:48.430 --> 00:04:52.570
I had watched the entire series, and then I got a phone call that they were doing an

150
00:04:52.570 --> 00:04:57.450
American version and asking me to audition for The Receptionist, and I about pooped myself.

151
00:04:57.570 --> 00:04:57.790
Yeah.

152
00:04:57.790 --> 00:04:58.330
Yes.

153
00:04:58.490 --> 00:05:03.270
I was excited and nervous because I loved the British version so much, and I was like,

154
00:05:03.390 --> 00:05:04.530
oh, I hope they do it justice.

155
00:05:04.830 --> 00:05:06.050
And I mean, come on.

156
00:05:06.770 --> 00:05:09.070
Years later, I think we did it.

157
00:05:09.250 --> 00:05:13.910
Well, we got a lot of critical attention when we were making the pilot, and not a lot

158
00:05:13.910 --> 00:05:17.390
of love because people really thought we were going to mess it up.

159
00:05:17.630 --> 00:05:17.950
That's right.

160
00:05:17.950 --> 00:05:22.990
People were worried because the British show was award-winning, critically acclaimed.

161
00:05:24.010 --> 00:05:28.970
It's considered still, to this day, one of the greatest television comedies ever made.

162
00:05:29.310 --> 00:05:30.930
And it deserves all of that.

163
00:05:31.010 --> 00:05:31.290
It is.

164
00:05:31.350 --> 00:05:32.590
Because it is amazing.

165
00:05:32.830 --> 00:05:33.130
Yes.

166
00:05:33.310 --> 00:05:36.550
And so there was a lot of pressure on us when we were going in to make this.

167
00:05:36.790 --> 00:05:42.250
And something that I think is often said is that we did a shot-by-shot remake of the

168
00:05:42.250 --> 00:05:43.970
British pilot, which is not true.

169
00:05:44.110 --> 00:05:44.790
That is not true.

170
00:05:44.790 --> 00:05:50.650
It is not a shot-for-shot remake, but we did use their script as a template, and there

171
00:05:50.650 --> 00:05:51.550
are some adjustments.

172
00:05:51.890 --> 00:05:52.890
There are elements, though.

173
00:05:52.950 --> 00:05:56.710
There are elements that are the same that if you watch it and watch this one.

174
00:05:57.450 --> 00:05:57.830
Yes.

175
00:05:57.890 --> 00:06:03.550
For example, in general, each of the characters and the fact that there's this buffoonish,

176
00:06:03.690 --> 00:06:07.890
silly boss in their version named David Brent and ours, Michael Scott.

177
00:06:08.190 --> 00:06:11.430
But if you look at the talking heads, there are some differences.

178
00:06:11.790 --> 00:06:12.070
Yes.

179
00:06:12.070 --> 00:06:14.930
And also, like, the way the story is told is a little different.

180
00:06:15.090 --> 00:06:19.550
I mean, some of the points are the same that happened, but it's told differently.

181
00:06:19.790 --> 00:06:25.510
But Greg Daniels was the person who was tasked with Americanizing the British show.

182
00:06:25.670 --> 00:06:26.110
Yes.

183
00:06:26.350 --> 00:06:30.450
And Ricky Gervais and Stephen Merchant kind of oversaw that process.

184
00:06:30.450 --> 00:06:34.130
But they, for example, they were not on set when we were shooting the pilot.

185
00:06:34.450 --> 00:06:34.810
Nope.

186
00:06:35.050 --> 00:06:35.210
No.

187
00:06:35.330 --> 00:06:37.190
They came and they visited with us.

188
00:06:37.350 --> 00:06:41.550
I remember sitting and getting to have lunch with Ricky Gervais.

189
00:06:41.550 --> 00:06:42.170
Yeah.

190
00:06:42.790 --> 00:06:43.830
Oh, my gosh.

191
00:06:43.950 --> 00:06:45.130
Were you nervous?

192
00:06:45.330 --> 00:06:45.930
Oh, yes.

193
00:06:46.030 --> 00:06:48.510
I was a super fan of the British version.

194
00:06:48.570 --> 00:06:48.990
Me, too.

195
00:06:49.170 --> 00:06:50.590
And I had been cast in the pilot.

196
00:06:50.910 --> 00:06:53.850
And they came and they listened to us do a table read.

197
00:06:54.030 --> 00:06:59.610
And afterwards, they asked me, John, Rain, Steve, and BJ, and Greg to join them for lunch

198
00:06:59.610 --> 00:07:01.870
to discuss the show.

199
00:07:01.870 --> 00:07:08.770
So, and I remember thinking, if I get fired tomorrow, at least I got to have lunch with

200
00:07:08.770 --> 00:07:10.630
Ricky Gervais and Stephen Merchant.

201
00:07:10.950 --> 00:07:12.310
At least that happened.

202
00:07:12.310 --> 00:07:14.970
That was, to me, just huge.

203
00:07:15.190 --> 00:07:16.030
Was it a fun lunch?

204
00:07:16.110 --> 00:07:17.150
I was not at that lunch.

205
00:07:17.210 --> 00:07:17.990
I was not invited.

206
00:07:18.850 --> 00:07:21.290
It was so much fun, Angela.

207
00:07:21.570 --> 00:07:24.550
And I remember Ricky saying something in that lunch that was really important.

208
00:07:24.750 --> 00:07:30.510
He said, you know, in England, you can be really, really bad at your job for a long time

209
00:07:30.510 --> 00:07:31.810
and you never get fired.

210
00:07:32.450 --> 00:07:35.250
He's like, in America, that's going to frustrate people.

211
00:07:35.450 --> 00:07:38.690
So, my one piece of advice is that Michael can be a buffoon.

212
00:07:38.830 --> 00:07:39.530
He can be silly.

213
00:07:39.690 --> 00:07:40.530
He can be irritating.

214
00:07:40.770 --> 00:07:46.270
But you should, I suggest, he said that you show glimpses of him actually being a good

215
00:07:46.270 --> 00:07:48.890
salesperson and we do that throughout the show.

216
00:07:49.110 --> 00:07:55.270
You see in future episodes that he is actually good at sales.

217
00:07:55.670 --> 00:07:58.790
And he said, that's going to be an important, I think, piece of the puzzle.

218
00:07:59.030 --> 00:08:05.670
And Stephen Merchant said, my piece of advice would be to lean a little bit more into the

219
00:08:05.670 --> 00:08:07.010
Jim and Pam relationship.

220
00:08:07.170 --> 00:08:09.450
He said, he knew the heart of it.

221
00:08:09.550 --> 00:08:11.830
He said, that's really going to be the heart of your show.

222
00:08:11.970 --> 00:08:15.690
Because the British version, they did six episodes for their first season.

223
00:08:15.690 --> 00:08:19.370
Six episodes for their second season and one big Christmas special.

224
00:08:20.050 --> 00:08:24.270
And they knew that for American television, we were looking to do years.

225
00:08:24.610 --> 00:08:26.370
And we ended up doing 200.

226
00:08:26.830 --> 00:08:29.970
So, he said, you need to keep the heart beating on the show.

227
00:08:30.110 --> 00:08:32.610
And so, that's the heart of the show.

228
00:08:32.770 --> 00:08:33.370
That's Jim and Pam.

229
00:08:33.730 --> 00:08:35.050
Okay, I have another fast fact.

230
00:08:35.270 --> 00:08:39.630
We filmed the pilot six months before the rest of season one.

231
00:08:40.050 --> 00:08:44.930
And we shot it on the second floor of a real office building in Culver City.

232
00:08:44.930 --> 00:08:47.130
So, it was an actual office building.

233
00:08:47.470 --> 00:08:50.050
And a lot of times on sets, they build the office.

234
00:08:50.050 --> 00:08:53.570
So, if they want to film and get a certain camera angle, they can move a wall.

235
00:08:53.750 --> 00:08:54.450
They can move a window.

236
00:08:54.570 --> 00:08:55.990
They can move a door because they built it.

237
00:08:56.110 --> 00:08:58.310
They actually say, wall flying out.

238
00:08:58.490 --> 00:08:59.550
And they move a whole wall.

239
00:08:59.610 --> 00:09:00.490
They move a whole wall.

240
00:09:00.690 --> 00:09:00.930
It's insane.

241
00:09:01.230 --> 00:09:05.110
On our show, because it was in an actual office building, you just had to work with it.

242
00:09:05.110 --> 00:09:10.010
And it sort of really lended itself to the documentary style that all the shots weren't going to look pretty.

243
00:09:10.210 --> 00:09:14.950
They weren't going to be lined up just right because they had to work around actual walls and windows.

244
00:09:15.130 --> 00:09:16.430
They couldn't just pop them out.

245
00:09:16.430 --> 00:09:23.790
I remember speaking to Ken Kwapis, our director, about setting certain rules for the documentary style of filming.

246
00:09:24.010 --> 00:09:34.790
And he said it was very important to him that for all of the actors and the crew that the set felt like a real working office and a real documentary was happening.

247
00:09:34.790 --> 00:09:45.130
And so, one of the rules that he came up with was that he cleared the entire set of all crew members except for a camera operator, a boom operator, and himself.

248
00:09:45.390 --> 00:09:45.730
Yes.

249
00:09:45.850 --> 00:09:46.530
And that was it.

250
00:09:46.570 --> 00:09:47.030
That was it.

251
00:09:47.050 --> 00:09:53.970
He also made all of the actors come in every morning, go through hair and makeup, and then sit at our desks starting at 7.30 a.m.

252
00:09:54.050 --> 00:09:55.670
And we had to pretend to work.

253
00:09:56.230 --> 00:09:56.670
Yes.

254
00:09:56.890 --> 00:10:00.530
And he walked around with the documentary crew and filmed us for 30 minutes.

255
00:10:00.710 --> 00:10:02.430
Yeah, just us quietly working.

256
00:10:02.430 --> 00:10:10.670
And little shots that you see, for example, of Pam doing whiteout, that is a piece of B-roll from one of those working mornings that we did.

257
00:10:10.730 --> 00:10:12.350
And we did that every day on the pilot.

258
00:10:12.790 --> 00:10:20.250
Something else about this episode that I noticed is that there is a random exterior shot of a building.

259
00:10:20.470 --> 00:10:21.930
Oh, Jenna, let me tell you.

260
00:10:22.070 --> 00:10:22.570
Well, wait, wait.

261
00:10:22.630 --> 00:10:25.170
Let me grab one of my colorful note cards, as you call them.

262
00:10:25.630 --> 00:10:29.410
Exterior shot at 9.29, 9 minutes and 29 seconds.

263
00:10:29.610 --> 00:10:30.590
Not our building.

264
00:10:30.590 --> 00:10:32.250
With a huge exclamation point.

265
00:10:32.250 --> 00:10:32.430
See?

266
00:10:32.490 --> 00:10:33.510
This was a big discovery.

267
00:10:33.710 --> 00:10:35.150
This was a big discovery for me.

268
00:10:35.690 --> 00:10:40.170
Not only not our building, it wasn't the building we were filming in.

269
00:10:40.350 --> 00:10:41.450
I don't know where they got that shot.

270
00:10:41.450 --> 00:10:41.890
I don't know.

271
00:10:41.990 --> 00:10:43.370
It's like stock footage from something?

272
00:10:43.450 --> 00:10:44.550
I think it was stock footage.

273
00:10:44.710 --> 00:10:46.330
And then we never use it again.

274
00:10:46.810 --> 00:10:47.450
That was it.

275
00:10:47.570 --> 00:10:48.070
That was it.

276
00:10:48.150 --> 00:10:49.670
They should go back and take that out.

277
00:10:49.750 --> 00:10:50.450
See you later.

278
00:10:50.630 --> 00:10:52.730
And put a shot of the reels under Mifflin exterior.

279
00:10:53.090 --> 00:10:54.330
Jenna's getting sassy on you.

280
00:10:54.350 --> 00:10:55.290
Yeah, I'm going to call someone.

281
00:10:55.390 --> 00:10:56.830
Someone get it right.

282
00:10:56.830 --> 00:10:57.710
All right.

283
00:10:57.870 --> 00:11:00.670
Here is my last fast fact for this episode.

284
00:11:00.930 --> 00:11:01.450
Okay, lady.

285
00:11:01.450 --> 00:11:03.550
Meredith is not Meredith.

286
00:11:03.790 --> 00:11:04.330
She is not.

287
00:11:04.510 --> 00:11:05.270
She is Henriette.

288
00:11:05.350 --> 00:11:06.210
It's Henriette.

289
00:11:06.450 --> 00:11:06.810
Yes.

290
00:11:06.990 --> 00:11:12.750
Meredith is played by Henriette Mantell in the pilot episode, Not Kate Flannery.

291
00:11:13.290 --> 00:11:13.910
This is true.

292
00:11:14.150 --> 00:11:21.110
Henriette Mantell was a comedy actress who had just broken out because she had played Alice in the Brady Bunch filMs.

293
00:11:21.630 --> 00:11:23.770
And she was there for the pilot.

294
00:11:23.770 --> 00:11:27.950
And then it was six months later that we got picked up.

295
00:11:27.990 --> 00:11:30.590
And then we started filming the rest of the episodes.

296
00:11:30.830 --> 00:11:33.310
And I guess the rumor was she was not available.

297
00:11:33.650 --> 00:11:33.950
She was busy.

298
00:11:34.090 --> 00:11:34.990
And we had to recast her.

299
00:11:35.230 --> 00:11:36.570
And so Kate Flannery came in.

300
00:11:36.650 --> 00:11:37.870
Here is my fast fact.

301
00:11:38.070 --> 00:11:41.470
Kate Flannery and I met years before the office.

302
00:11:41.470 --> 00:11:45.830
We were in an all-female improv group called Bitch Planet.

303
00:11:46.250 --> 00:11:46.910
Woo-hoo!

304
00:11:47.330 --> 00:11:47.970
Aw, Bitch Planet.

305
00:11:47.970 --> 00:11:49.950
Sunday nights at like 10 p.m.

306
00:11:49.990 --> 00:11:50.650
That's when you want to.

307
00:11:50.650 --> 00:11:52.230
It's exactly when you want your comedy.

308
00:11:52.370 --> 00:11:53.090
That's when you want to watch improv.

309
00:11:53.390 --> 00:11:55.190
Kate and I had known each other for a really long time.

310
00:11:55.330 --> 00:12:00.210
And also, Oscar Nunez and I did a sketch comedy show together called Hot Towel.

311
00:12:00.330 --> 00:12:01.970
Where do you get these names, Angela?

312
00:12:02.130 --> 00:12:04.270
So it was really fun for me because here I am.

313
00:12:04.410 --> 00:12:06.350
Like, I was so nervous the first day.

314
00:12:06.510 --> 00:12:08.270
I didn't know Oscar had been caSt.

315
00:12:08.390 --> 00:12:09.630
He didn't know I had been caSt.

316
00:12:09.890 --> 00:12:11.170
You guys, this was sort of before.

317
00:12:11.310 --> 00:12:12.530
This was before smartphones.

318
00:12:12.730 --> 00:12:17.290
Like, it's not like you, like, texted someone you got a role or you put it on your social media.

319
00:12:17.430 --> 00:12:18.630
There was none of that.

320
00:12:18.630 --> 00:12:20.950
So you walked in and were completely surprised.

321
00:12:21.170 --> 00:12:23.030
I was completely surprised when I saw Oscar.

322
00:12:23.450 --> 00:12:28.190
And not only did I see Oscar, but Ken Coapa sat us next to each other.

323
00:12:28.270 --> 00:12:30.250
So I was like, oh my God, I'm going to be okay.

324
00:12:30.590 --> 00:12:31.270
That's amazing.

325
00:12:31.470 --> 00:12:32.230
My buddy's here.

326
00:12:32.470 --> 00:12:34.930
And then Brian walked in as Kevin and I was like, here we go.

327
00:12:35.050 --> 00:12:35.810
We're off to the races.

328
00:12:36.090 --> 00:12:37.650
You guys were amazing in accounting.

329
00:12:38.030 --> 00:12:38.650
Thank you.

330
00:12:38.830 --> 00:12:39.810
Yeah, thank you so much.

331
00:12:39.970 --> 00:12:41.030
I'm giving you a compliment.

332
00:12:41.250 --> 00:12:41.530
Thank you so much.

333
00:12:41.530 --> 00:12:46.050
So yeah, so I was so excited to have these friends that I had performed with for years

334
00:12:46.050 --> 00:12:48.450
before the office sitting in my little corner back there.

335
00:12:49.150 --> 00:12:51.450
I remember meeting everyone for the first time.

336
00:12:51.570 --> 00:12:53.210
Do you remember your first impressions of people?

337
00:12:53.810 --> 00:12:54.150
I do.

338
00:12:54.150 --> 00:12:55.890
I remember my first impression of Rainn Wilson.

339
00:12:56.170 --> 00:12:56.690
Tell me.

340
00:12:56.910 --> 00:13:00.810
Well, I met Rainn Wilson at a live test audition.

341
00:13:01.190 --> 00:13:05.770
So for all the leads of the show, they brought us into screen test and they mixed and matched

342
00:13:05.770 --> 00:13:06.050
us.

343
00:13:06.150 --> 00:13:09.490
There were four Pams and four Dwights and four Jims and four Michaels.

344
00:13:09.670 --> 00:13:12.730
And we spent an entire day being mixed and matched with each other.

345
00:13:12.930 --> 00:13:18.510
And I remember I met Rainn Wilson and he was in character, but I couldn't really know

346
00:13:18.510 --> 00:13:21.070
that because I didn't know Rainn yet.

347
00:13:21.630 --> 00:13:25.930
And I thought he was the weirdest person I'd ever met in my life.

348
00:13:25.950 --> 00:13:27.550
Wait, he was in character the whole time?

349
00:13:27.730 --> 00:13:27.930
Yeah.

350
00:13:28.170 --> 00:13:29.270
He just was Dwight.

351
00:13:29.310 --> 00:13:30.710
Oh, so method, Rainn.

352
00:13:30.710 --> 00:13:31.530
He was in character like that.

353
00:13:31.910 --> 00:13:32.710
So method.

354
00:13:32.710 --> 00:13:33.490
But you know what?

355
00:13:33.570 --> 00:13:35.170
I kind of did the same thing.

356
00:13:35.910 --> 00:13:37.930
I did my Pam hairstyle.

357
00:13:38.270 --> 00:13:42.870
The hairstyle that I wore on the show, I sort of invented through the audition process.

358
00:13:42.870 --> 00:13:43.070
Right.

359
00:13:43.070 --> 00:13:47.410
That sort of half up, half down, scrunchy, air-dried curl.

360
00:13:47.650 --> 00:13:49.810
And so I was very quiet.

361
00:13:49.810 --> 00:13:54.750
I didn't talk to people in the waiting room because I just thought Pam was sort of not a

362
00:13:54.750 --> 00:13:56.850
chatter and I didn't want to get out of character.

363
00:13:57.030 --> 00:13:58.690
I remember one of the first things.

364
00:13:58.690 --> 00:14:01.790
Wait, this is so random, but it's true.

365
00:14:02.030 --> 00:14:06.030
That first week of filming, Rainn's wife came to have lunch.

366
00:14:06.170 --> 00:14:12.050
And this is the main interaction I remember from Rainn during the pilot episode.

367
00:14:13.150 --> 00:14:14.710
Rainn introduced me to his wife.

368
00:14:14.930 --> 00:14:17.010
She was pregnant at the time with their son.

369
00:14:17.170 --> 00:14:17.450
Yes.

370
00:14:17.450 --> 00:14:21.130
And he said, this is my wife, Holly, bearer of my seed.

371
00:14:25.480 --> 00:14:26.900
And she started laughing.

372
00:14:27.240 --> 00:14:29.080
But that was just Rain being Rain.

373
00:14:29.400 --> 00:14:29.540
That's Rain.

374
00:14:29.760 --> 00:14:30.320
That is Rain.

375
00:14:30.400 --> 00:14:31.620
But wouldn't Dwight say that?

376
00:14:31.680 --> 00:14:32.900
He would also say that.

377
00:14:33.000 --> 00:14:34.100
This is Angela, bearer of my seed.

378
00:14:34.260 --> 00:14:34.620
Yes.

379
00:14:34.740 --> 00:14:35.640
It's amazing how...

380
00:14:35.640 --> 00:14:36.800
She's carrying my seed.

381
00:14:36.800 --> 00:14:38.080
I'm like, oh my God.

382
00:14:38.080 --> 00:14:41.820
I feel like we all had little parts of our characters in us.

383
00:14:41.880 --> 00:14:45.540
But I don't know if that's true of you because you are so opposite from Angela Martin.

384
00:14:45.700 --> 00:14:46.060
I am.

385
00:14:46.140 --> 00:14:52.160
I was thinking about how you were saying, like, you stayed in character and so did Rainn in between your auditions in the lobby.

386
00:14:52.240 --> 00:14:53.340
I would not have.

387
00:14:53.340 --> 00:14:59.540
I would have been like, chitty-chat-chat, chitty-chat-chat, and then I would have just turned into a bitch when I entered the room.

388
00:14:59.860 --> 00:15:04.700
Well, you know, I didn't have any scenes with Rainn in the pilot of Substance.

389
00:15:04.820 --> 00:15:05.940
I didn't have any one-on-one scenes.

390
00:15:05.940 --> 00:15:15.060
So for me, when we were shooting the pilot, the people that I really bonded with were Steve, John, and you because you were over the little part.

391
00:15:15.160 --> 00:15:17.520
You were my closest seatmate in the office.

392
00:15:17.780 --> 00:15:18.960
So you and I bonded.

393
00:15:18.960 --> 00:15:28.240
And I remember after we finished the pilot and we all left and we didn't know if we were going to be picked up or make any more, shortly after that, I turned 30 years old.

394
00:15:28.440 --> 00:15:30.420
It was a big milestone birthday for me.

395
00:15:31.100 --> 00:15:34.280
And I really wondered if I should invite any of you guys.

396
00:15:34.640 --> 00:15:35.800
And I didn't.

397
00:15:35.920 --> 00:15:40.420
I didn't invite you guys because I thought, well, I'm probably never going to see them again.

398
00:15:40.700 --> 00:15:44.160
Should I throw you another 30th birthday party and invite everyone?

399
00:15:44.640 --> 00:15:45.340
Would you?

400
00:15:45.580 --> 00:15:47.760
And then we could just say happy 30th.

401
00:15:47.940 --> 00:15:48.660
Will you?

402
00:15:49.300 --> 00:15:49.700
Okay.

403
00:15:50.740 --> 00:15:55.400
Because now you guys are some of my closest friends and you aren't at my milestone birthday.

404
00:15:55.600 --> 00:15:55.860
Aw.

405
00:15:56.480 --> 00:15:58.600
Okay, we'll have to throw you a 30th birthday party.

406
00:15:58.760 --> 00:15:59.500
It won't be weird.

407
00:15:59.900 --> 00:16:00.700
All right, guys.

408
00:16:00.840 --> 00:16:04.160
So next up, we are going to break down this episode.

409
00:16:04.320 --> 00:16:05.820
We're going to really get into the nitty gritty.

410
00:16:05.960 --> 00:16:08.260
We're going to talk about scenes and memories and stories.

411
00:16:08.400 --> 00:16:09.100
So stick with us.

412
00:16:12.820 --> 00:16:15.200
We're talking about the pilot episode of The Office.

413
00:16:15.340 --> 00:16:18.220
And I think we should talk about some of our favorite moments, Jenna.

414
00:16:18.460 --> 00:16:18.820
Yes.

415
00:16:18.820 --> 00:16:23.040
Well, one of my favorite moments is the dynamic between Pam and Michael.

416
00:16:23.340 --> 00:16:25.300
I mean, selfishly, I guess I'm in that moment.

417
00:16:25.460 --> 00:16:25.660
Yeah.

418
00:16:25.660 --> 00:16:28.220
But I really loved working with Steve Carell.

419
00:16:28.400 --> 00:16:33.020
I really loved how much he let me be silent and just look at him.

420
00:16:33.020 --> 00:16:33.880
And just react.

421
00:16:34.060 --> 00:16:34.400
Yes.

422
00:16:34.500 --> 00:16:35.220
I loved that.

423
00:16:35.380 --> 00:16:41.260
But I especially love whenever Melora Hardin, who plays Jan Levinson Gould, got in the mix.

424
00:16:41.340 --> 00:16:42.560
She's so commanding.

425
00:16:42.780 --> 00:16:45.780
Just like Melora's presence is like, I'm scared of her.

426
00:16:45.900 --> 00:16:46.240
Yes.

427
00:16:46.240 --> 00:16:46.880
I get it.

428
00:16:47.020 --> 00:16:48.880
I get why he was a little scared of her.

429
00:16:49.300 --> 00:16:56.100
And I love the scene where Michael tries to throw Pam under the bus about why he doesn't have the agenda that he's supposed to have.

430
00:16:56.220 --> 00:17:02.500
And it's because he told her to throw it in the receptacle, the trash can that is a special filing cabinet for corporate memos.

431
00:17:02.500 --> 00:17:14.360
And I remember shooting that scene, and I just remember every time that Pam said, you told me to put it in a special filing cabinet that is a trash can, that we would giggle.

432
00:17:15.160 --> 00:17:21.320
And so I just remember the matter-of-factness of that moment and how fun that was.

433
00:17:21.420 --> 00:17:24.420
Pam totally stood up for herself in her own little way in that moment.

434
00:17:24.640 --> 00:17:26.020
Here's the thing about Steve Carell.

435
00:17:26.160 --> 00:17:28.780
He is one of the nicest people you will ever meet.

436
00:17:28.800 --> 00:17:30.200
He is so nice.

437
00:17:30.200 --> 00:17:35.060
I know it's almost even become like a joke, like Steve Carell's the nicest person ever, but he is.

438
00:17:35.220 --> 00:17:35.460
I know.

439
00:17:35.520 --> 00:17:38.100
And people ask me a lot, what's it like to work with Steve Carell?

440
00:17:38.180 --> 00:17:39.820
Are you just laughing all the time?

441
00:17:40.560 --> 00:17:44.220
And no, you're not laughing all the time because Steve isn't on all the time.

442
00:17:44.360 --> 00:17:45.180
He's not one of those.

443
00:17:45.280 --> 00:17:45.660
He's a real person.

444
00:17:45.660 --> 00:17:46.520
He's a real person.

445
00:17:46.620 --> 00:17:50.160
And he's, he's, I've worked with comedians who are on all the time.

446
00:17:50.300 --> 00:17:54.380
You walk around and you can just, they can't turn off their joke-making brain.

447
00:17:54.560 --> 00:17:55.280
I drive my cursor.

448
00:17:55.520 --> 00:17:56.560
It's a little insane.

449
00:17:56.980 --> 00:17:58.140
Steve is not like that.

450
00:17:58.140 --> 00:18:03.200
But you and Steve and I would sit and have long conversations about the best frozen

451
00:18:03.200 --> 00:18:04.780
yogurt in the San Fernando Valley.

452
00:18:04.980 --> 00:18:09.960
Oh, I will never forget the morning, on Monday mornings, Steve would come out of his office,

453
00:18:10.160 --> 00:18:12.240
out of Michael Scott's office between scenes.

454
00:18:12.420 --> 00:18:15.540
He would walk over to Jenna and I, and we would talk about our weekends.

455
00:18:15.740 --> 00:18:19.980
And one Monday morning, he walked over and he was like, ladies, guess what I did this weekend?

456
00:18:20.080 --> 00:18:20.460
We're like, what?

457
00:18:20.520 --> 00:18:21.680
And he goes, I went to Target.

458
00:18:21.680 --> 00:18:21.800
Target.

459
00:18:22.500 --> 00:18:23.700
He would talk to us about Target.

460
00:18:23.700 --> 00:18:25.340
And he told us about his Target run.

461
00:18:25.600 --> 00:18:27.380
It was really, really sweet.

462
00:18:27.640 --> 00:18:33.800
You know, recently, I was in a Michaels purchasing some supplies for a crafting job.

463
00:18:33.940 --> 00:18:39.240
And the checkout guy said, oh my gosh, now this is getting crazy.

464
00:18:39.500 --> 00:18:42.060
This morning, Oscar Nunez came in here.

465
00:18:42.240 --> 00:18:43.480
Then Steve Carell.

466
00:18:43.600 --> 00:18:44.280
And now you?

467
00:18:44.480 --> 00:18:45.680
What do you guys all live here?

468
00:18:45.900 --> 00:18:47.500
We love crafting.

469
00:18:47.920 --> 00:18:48.900
We love crafting.

470
00:18:48.900 --> 00:18:50.220
And we're all here.

471
00:18:50.460 --> 00:18:54.460
We're all sort of, I guess we all live around this one Michaels crafting store.

472
00:18:54.580 --> 00:18:54.700
Yeah.

473
00:18:54.820 --> 00:18:59.820
I texted Steve and I said, Steve, the checkout guy at Michaels told me you were buying some

474
00:18:59.820 --> 00:19:01.080
crafting supplies today.

475
00:19:01.220 --> 00:19:03.860
And he said, oh yes, I needed some glue sticks.

476
00:19:04.060 --> 00:19:05.620
And he sort of told me what he needed.

477
00:19:05.660 --> 00:19:06.900
He's like, what were you there for?

478
00:19:07.440 --> 00:19:09.020
But that is Steve.

479
00:19:09.200 --> 00:19:09.840
That is Steve.

480
00:19:09.960 --> 00:19:11.820
That's the kind of stuff we would talk to him about.

481
00:19:11.980 --> 00:19:18.200
And so to then, to have that man then shift into this scene where he's just being awful

482
00:19:18.200 --> 00:19:19.080
to my character.

483
00:19:19.480 --> 00:19:22.460
Like throwing you under the bus in front of the big boss lady.

484
00:19:22.500 --> 00:19:22.800
Yes.

485
00:19:22.900 --> 00:19:25.940
When our real dynamic was just like so much.

486
00:19:25.940 --> 00:19:26.500
So different.

487
00:19:26.940 --> 00:19:27.160
So much love.

488
00:19:27.440 --> 00:19:31.500
So in the scene, Jan tells Michael there's going to be downsizing.

489
00:19:31.600 --> 00:19:31.700
Yeah.

490
00:19:31.820 --> 00:19:32.860
This is big news.

491
00:19:33.000 --> 00:19:33.400
Big news.

492
00:19:33.620 --> 00:19:33.980
This is important.

493
00:19:34.820 --> 00:19:36.120
Michael should be paying attention.

494
00:19:36.400 --> 00:19:39.200
We've already established that he's thrown away the agenda for the meeting.

495
00:19:39.380 --> 00:19:39.520
Yeah.

496
00:19:39.560 --> 00:19:40.620
He should take this seriously.

497
00:19:40.840 --> 00:19:44.100
But instead, he takes a phone call from his friend, Todd Packer.

498
00:19:44.100 --> 00:19:48.100
Todd Packer, his party buddy, who he clearly covets.

499
00:19:48.280 --> 00:19:49.520
He thinks this guy's amazing.

500
00:19:49.740 --> 00:19:55.760
Another interesting fact about this scene is that the voice of Todd Packer that comes

501
00:19:55.760 --> 00:20:01.280
on the phone is not the voice of the real Todd Packer.

502
00:20:01.440 --> 00:20:03.020
What are you talking about?

503
00:20:03.260 --> 00:20:06.300
Dave Koechner ends up playing Todd Packer.

504
00:20:06.440 --> 00:20:10.920
When we meet him in season two, this role is played by Dave Koechner.

505
00:20:10.920 --> 00:20:14.560
But Dave was not hired when we shot the pilot.

506
00:20:14.700 --> 00:20:14.880
What?

507
00:20:15.020 --> 00:20:15.360
He's not?

508
00:20:15.500 --> 00:20:15.660
Wait.

509
00:20:15.800 --> 00:20:18.300
So I was thinking, it sounds like Dave Koechner.

510
00:20:18.300 --> 00:20:18.860
Who is it?

511
00:20:18.860 --> 00:20:21.100
But I'm thinking it can't be Dave Koechner, right?

512
00:20:21.100 --> 00:20:22.960
It's all this time I thought it was Dave Koechner.

513
00:20:23.080 --> 00:20:23.880
It's not Dave.

514
00:20:24.000 --> 00:20:24.400
Who is it?

515
00:20:24.480 --> 00:20:27.320
I texted Dave Rogers, our editor.

516
00:20:27.600 --> 00:20:27.940
Dave Rogers.

517
00:20:28.180 --> 00:20:29.200
Knower of all things.

518
00:20:29.280 --> 00:20:31.260
I mean, he is knower of all office facts.

519
00:20:31.260 --> 00:20:31.660
He is.

520
00:20:31.860 --> 00:20:32.040
Okay.

521
00:20:32.120 --> 00:20:32.980
What did Dave say?

522
00:20:33.080 --> 00:20:37.600
Dave said that the voice is played by Toby Huss.

523
00:20:37.860 --> 00:20:38.780
Toby Huss.

524
00:20:38.800 --> 00:20:38.960
Yes.

525
00:20:39.160 --> 00:20:40.660
Very famous voice actor.

526
00:20:40.660 --> 00:20:42.300
He was on King of the Hill for years.

527
00:20:42.480 --> 00:20:43.700
And Friends of Greg Daniels.

528
00:20:43.700 --> 00:20:43.940
Yes.

529
00:20:44.300 --> 00:20:45.540
Toby Huss is so talented.

530
00:20:45.680 --> 00:20:45.980
I know.

531
00:20:46.100 --> 00:20:46.800
How about that?

532
00:20:46.980 --> 00:20:48.920
That is Todd Packer's voice.

533
00:20:49.120 --> 00:20:50.240
In the pilot episode.

534
00:20:50.400 --> 00:20:50.620
Yes.

535
00:20:51.100 --> 00:20:55.720
Well, I loved, here's one of the things I loved about the pilot, and just sort of the brilliance

536
00:20:55.720 --> 00:20:59.640
that was Ricky Gervais and Stephen Merchant and Greg Daniels and their collaboration building

537
00:20:59.640 --> 00:21:05.860
this world out, is that so many, like, truths about the characters were established in all

538
00:21:05.860 --> 00:21:06.800
these subtle ways.

539
00:21:06.820 --> 00:21:12.620
And one of the things I loved is that Michael is clearly thrilled that the camera people

540
00:21:12.620 --> 00:21:13.120
are there.

541
00:21:13.320 --> 00:21:13.600
Oh.

542
00:21:13.700 --> 00:21:14.580
He is so excited.

543
00:21:14.700 --> 00:21:16.460
He could not have signed up for this faster.

544
00:21:16.460 --> 00:21:18.660
I mean, he jumped at the chance at this.

545
00:21:18.760 --> 00:21:21.820
And he thinks he is one of the world's best comedians.

546
00:21:21.880 --> 00:21:22.280
Oh, yeah.

547
00:21:22.380 --> 00:21:22.800
In the world.

548
00:21:22.900 --> 00:21:24.820
He is one of the funniest people in the world.

549
00:21:24.920 --> 00:21:28.880
He has that amazing talking head where he talks about who his heroes are.

550
00:21:28.960 --> 00:21:29.320
Yes.

551
00:21:29.580 --> 00:21:31.320
And they are Bob Hope.

552
00:21:31.520 --> 00:21:32.520
Abraham Lincoln.

553
00:21:33.080 --> 00:21:33.560
Bono.

554
00:21:33.800 --> 00:21:34.380
And God.

555
00:21:34.580 --> 00:21:35.000
Probably.

556
00:21:35.260 --> 00:21:36.120
He said probably.

557
00:21:36.120 --> 00:21:36.840
Probably God.

558
00:21:37.080 --> 00:21:38.740
And here's a little tidbit.

559
00:21:39.180 --> 00:21:39.900
Steve wrote that.

560
00:21:40.080 --> 00:21:40.600
He did?

561
00:21:40.760 --> 00:21:41.020
Yes.

562
00:21:41.020 --> 00:21:41.680
Oh, my God.

563
00:21:41.680 --> 00:21:42.180
I love that.

564
00:21:42.240 --> 00:21:42.940
I didn't know that.

565
00:21:43.160 --> 00:21:43.300
Yeah.

566
00:21:43.480 --> 00:21:49.720
Greg asked him, would you mind writing a list of who you believe are Michael Scott's

567
00:21:49.720 --> 00:21:50.120
heroes?

568
00:21:50.240 --> 00:21:51.140
Oh, so brilliant.

569
00:21:51.340 --> 00:21:51.780
Bob Hope.

570
00:21:51.800 --> 00:21:52.240
And it made it in.

571
00:21:52.360 --> 00:21:52.740
Yes.

572
00:21:53.180 --> 00:21:56.580
Well, and he just, every chance he gets, he's, like, looking to the camera and doing

573
00:21:56.580 --> 00:21:57.560
his comedy bits.

574
00:21:57.680 --> 00:22:02.800
And this sort of sets up just Michael's love of being liked and being appreciated.

575
00:22:03.200 --> 00:22:06.420
And he wants everyone to think he's, like, the fun boss.

576
00:22:06.460 --> 00:22:08.660
And he thinks he's legitimately talented.

577
00:22:08.660 --> 00:22:12.180
Well, there's that scene where he does the Six Million Dollar Man, where he's walking away

578
00:22:12.180 --> 00:22:12.980
from Pam's desk.

579
00:22:13.080 --> 00:22:13.400
Yes.

580
00:22:14.300 --> 00:22:16.300
I could not get through that scene.

581
00:22:16.440 --> 00:22:21.280
And in fact, in order to get my reaction shot, they had to make Steve leave the set

582
00:22:22.280 --> 00:22:24.160
because I couldn't get through it.

583
00:22:24.280 --> 00:22:29.820
So that reaction, yes, that reaction of me was not while Steve was actually doing anything

584
00:22:29.820 --> 00:22:32.180
because every time he did it, I lost it.

585
00:22:32.200 --> 00:22:32.260
You started laughing.

586
00:22:32.480 --> 00:22:32.620
Yeah.

587
00:22:32.760 --> 00:22:35.460
Well, he's so giddy to do his Six Million Dollar Man.

588
00:22:35.460 --> 00:22:40.980
I timed it because as I watched it, I was like, wow, this is a long time to commit to

589
00:22:40.980 --> 00:22:43.860
this bit because he's, like, fa-da-da-da-da-da-da-da-da-da-da-da.

590
00:22:44.260 --> 00:22:45.060
He did it.

591
00:22:45.180 --> 00:22:47.700
It started at 9 minutes, 40 seconds.

592
00:22:47.840 --> 00:22:50.380
And it doesn't end until 10 minutes, 2 seconds.

593
00:22:50.540 --> 00:22:51.200
Oh, my gosh.

594
00:22:51.200 --> 00:22:54.640
That is 22 seconds of the slow-mo bit.

595
00:22:54.960 --> 00:22:56.140
I think that's why.

596
00:22:56.240 --> 00:22:57.080
That's a ton.

597
00:22:57.080 --> 00:23:03.300
And when you think of, like, the whole scope of an episode, for 22 seconds, he went fa-da-da-da-da-da-da-da-da-da-da-da-da-da.

598
00:23:03.480 --> 00:23:05.800
I mean, that's probably why I couldn't get through it.

599
00:23:05.840 --> 00:23:09.320
That's why they couldn't get a reaction of me because I couldn't hold it for 20 seconds.

600
00:23:09.320 --> 00:23:11.060
I couldn't hold a straight face that long.

601
00:23:11.160 --> 00:23:14.920
And he was so desperate for you to look at him and have some kind of reaction.

602
00:23:15.580 --> 00:23:16.140
Oh, man.

603
00:23:16.380 --> 00:23:17.200
Okay, what else?

604
00:23:17.280 --> 00:23:17.620
What else?

605
00:23:17.900 --> 00:23:19.420
There's the conference room scene.

606
00:23:19.600 --> 00:23:20.220
Oh, yes.

607
00:23:20.220 --> 00:23:25.400
Where Michael officially announces that there is going to be downsizing at Dunder Mifflin.

608
00:23:25.480 --> 00:23:27.160
This is a big theme of the pilot.

609
00:23:27.440 --> 00:23:36.560
This is the news that Jan gives him when she comes into the office, that Dunder Mifflin can no longer support both a Scranton branch and a Stanford branch.

610
00:23:36.600 --> 00:23:41.880
And that they're going to have to prove who is the branch that should stay.

611
00:23:42.160 --> 00:23:47.120
But even if Scranton gets to stay, there might be downsizing of employees.

612
00:23:47.340 --> 00:23:48.380
Yeah, the company's hurting.

613
00:23:48.380 --> 00:23:51.160
And they have to make some cutbacks, and they have to fire some people.

614
00:23:51.460 --> 00:23:57.380
Yes, and so Michael goes into the conference room, and this is the first time we see Michael in the conference room.

615
00:23:57.500 --> 00:24:00.360
And, of course, Dwight won't give him the space to do that.

616
00:24:00.440 --> 00:24:03.400
It's their first power struggle, which you'll see for years to come.

617
00:24:03.560 --> 00:24:06.780
It's the first time he refers to himself as the assistant regional manager.

618
00:24:07.100 --> 00:24:07.500
Yeah.

619
00:24:07.820 --> 00:24:08.680
Which is great.

620
00:24:09.200 --> 00:24:11.440
And I noticed something about this conference room scene.

621
00:24:11.600 --> 00:24:11.960
What?

622
00:24:12.200 --> 00:24:13.780
I bet I have it on my note card.

623
00:24:13.780 --> 00:24:20.360
I bet you have it that there are some extras in this scene that are never to be seen again.

624
00:24:20.640 --> 00:24:21.460
There are two women.

625
00:24:21.660 --> 00:24:23.060
Do you want me to refer to it?

626
00:24:23.220 --> 00:24:25.020
Let me just tell you what I wrote on my note card.

627
00:24:25.180 --> 00:24:25.320
Okay.

628
00:24:25.560 --> 00:24:27.760
Conference room faces, semicolon.

629
00:24:29.340 --> 00:24:37.540
So, in the background, there are two women, and they are the real production company accountants for our show.

630
00:24:37.700 --> 00:24:43.800
Yes, we needed more people to fill out the space, and they pulled in our actual accountants from our production office.

631
00:24:43.940 --> 00:24:48.960
Yes, Ken Guapas, the director, walked into the production office and asked if anyone would like to be in the background of a scene.

632
00:24:49.100 --> 00:24:52.160
And those two women came and sat there for that couple of hours.

633
00:24:52.400 --> 00:24:53.460
They were so giddy.

634
00:24:53.640 --> 00:24:54.600
They loved it.

635
00:24:54.600 --> 00:24:55.240
They loved it.

636
00:24:55.360 --> 00:24:55.740
They loved it.

637
00:24:55.840 --> 00:25:03.200
And then, also, in the conference room, some faces you might see sprinkled through the first season, but then they don't go past that season, are Luann Kelly,

638
00:25:03.440 --> 00:25:04.840
who worked in the annex.

639
00:25:05.180 --> 00:25:05.340
Yes.

640
00:25:05.340 --> 00:25:06.540
She is someone that you see a lot.

641
00:25:06.680 --> 00:25:09.220
I get asked a lot, who is the lady in the annex?

642
00:25:09.400 --> 00:25:09.600
Yes.

643
00:25:09.640 --> 00:25:10.020
It's Luann.

644
00:25:10.020 --> 00:25:13.080
She was sort of over where Mindy ends up sitting.

645
00:25:13.380 --> 00:25:13.540
Yes.

646
00:25:13.620 --> 00:25:14.440
By Kelly's desk.

647
00:25:14.580 --> 00:25:20.080
And then, also, of course, Devin and Creed, who ultimately do have a storyline that will come later.

648
00:25:20.080 --> 00:25:20.940
But not until season two.

649
00:25:21.160 --> 00:25:21.560
That's right.

650
00:25:21.560 --> 00:25:26.180
So, for an entire season, you just see Devin and Creed in the background of scenes.

651
00:25:26.400 --> 00:25:28.300
They don't get any lines until the second season.

652
00:25:28.340 --> 00:25:32.120
But that is the only scene our actual accountants are in, because then they had to go back to work.

653
00:25:32.260 --> 00:25:32.640
They did.

654
00:25:32.840 --> 00:25:35.120
They had to actually go back to being accountants for the show.

655
00:25:35.300 --> 00:25:36.900
I think this is an interesting tidbit.

656
00:25:37.240 --> 00:25:43.980
Phyllis Smith, who plays Phyllis on the show, she was the casting associate for The Office.

657
00:25:43.980 --> 00:25:49.060
So, when I auditioned for The Office, she read my audition scenes with me.

658
00:25:49.440 --> 00:25:51.660
Everyone, I read my scene with Phyllis.

659
00:25:51.780 --> 00:25:52.980
That's who I read my scene with.

660
00:25:53.100 --> 00:25:53.400
Yes.

661
00:25:53.580 --> 00:25:58.220
And Ken Kwap, as the director, was so taken with how she did her readings with the actors.

662
00:25:58.420 --> 00:25:59.640
She's so earnest, you guys.

663
00:25:59.740 --> 00:26:00.900
She is so earneSt.

664
00:26:00.900 --> 00:26:04.560
And so real, that he said to Greg Daniels, I want her on the show.

665
00:26:05.060 --> 00:26:06.840
Can I give her a part on the show?

666
00:26:07.260 --> 00:26:14.540
And so, Phyllis went from being a casting associate, which she had done for years with Allison Jones, the casting director.

667
00:26:14.660 --> 00:26:14.820
Yes.

668
00:26:14.940 --> 00:26:18.640
Worked with Phyllis, to having a part on this pilot.

669
00:26:18.920 --> 00:26:23.400
And I think because we all thought the show was going nowhere, it was fine.

670
00:26:23.420 --> 00:26:26.020
And after the pilot was over, she went back to being a casting associate.

671
00:26:26.140 --> 00:26:27.060
She went right back to work.

672
00:26:27.560 --> 00:26:35.300
Phyllis has a great story that she tells that the way she found out that she got Phyllis is that she got sides and they said, Phyllis.

673
00:26:36.240 --> 00:26:38.080
Oh, so there was a character.

674
00:26:38.280 --> 00:26:38.440
Yeah.

675
00:26:38.600 --> 00:26:39.520
And they named it Phyllis.

676
00:26:39.560 --> 00:26:41.140
And Ken was like, that's you.

677
00:26:41.260 --> 00:26:41.880
You're Phyllis.

678
00:26:42.020 --> 00:26:42.800
And she was like, what?

679
00:26:43.720 --> 00:26:47.520
We wrote a part for you based on the numerous readings with actors.

680
00:26:47.740 --> 00:26:49.140
You're now in the show, Phyllis.

681
00:26:49.240 --> 00:26:53.900
Which is just so cool because Phyllis had wanted to be an actress.

682
00:26:54.200 --> 00:26:54.980
She had been an actress.

683
00:26:55.140 --> 00:26:56.220
She had been a burlesque dancer.

684
00:26:56.220 --> 00:26:56.700
Burlesque dancer.

685
00:26:56.700 --> 00:26:57.980
No one can shimmy.

686
00:26:58.120 --> 00:26:59.540
No one can shimmy like Phyllis.

687
00:26:59.780 --> 00:27:00.080
That's right.

688
00:27:00.100 --> 00:27:01.160
She has a great shimmy.

689
00:27:01.320 --> 00:27:08.360
She had an amazing photo on her desk because Ken Kwapis wanted us to personalize our desk for the pilot episode and moving forward.

690
00:27:08.560 --> 00:27:10.340
So we all brought in something from home.

691
00:27:10.480 --> 00:27:15.880
I have a photo of myself and my grandmother and a frame that says, I heart grandma.

692
00:27:16.200 --> 00:27:20.260
And my eyes are closed in the photo, but my grandmother framed it anyway.

693
00:27:21.240 --> 00:27:26.200
And so I have that on my desk and it just kind of like tickled me and made me think of my grandmother.

694
00:27:26.560 --> 00:27:29.160
I had a picture of me and my sister swimming in Lake of the Ozarks.

695
00:27:29.340 --> 00:27:29.680
Yes.

696
00:27:29.800 --> 00:27:37.420
And Phyllis had a picture of herself in her full burlesque outfit with a red feather boa draped over her shoulders.

697
00:27:37.820 --> 00:27:39.140
Angela, let's take a quick break.

698
00:27:39.380 --> 00:27:39.940
Okay, Jenna.

699
00:27:40.480 --> 00:27:41.500
You're going to get a snack.

700
00:27:41.900 --> 00:27:42.280
Why?

701
00:27:42.960 --> 00:27:45.140
Because you always get a snack during the breaks.

702
00:27:45.360 --> 00:27:45.680
I do.

703
00:27:48.000 --> 00:27:53.320
So then we get to that crazy scene where Michael is walking Ryan the temp around.

704
00:27:53.320 --> 00:27:56.660
So he's just been told that we need downsizing.

705
00:27:56.880 --> 00:27:58.440
And then he hires a temp.

706
00:27:59.480 --> 00:28:00.600
He hires them.

707
00:28:00.680 --> 00:28:02.240
It's the opposite of downsizing.

708
00:28:02.260 --> 00:28:02.920
It's the opposite.

709
00:28:03.180 --> 00:28:08.940
Can I just say one thing I love too is when Dwight has his talking head and he said, I've been talking about downsizing for years.

710
00:28:09.060 --> 00:28:10.180
I brought it up in my interview.

711
00:28:11.080 --> 00:28:12.440
Like, who does that?

712
00:28:12.540 --> 00:28:15.840
He was like interviewing for a job and is like, you should downsize.

713
00:28:16.680 --> 00:28:17.840
I would like this job.

714
00:28:17.840 --> 00:28:19.860
I suggest you get rid of three other people.

715
00:28:20.360 --> 00:28:22.320
So here's a little fun fact.

716
00:28:22.420 --> 00:28:23.760
It's not a fast fact.

717
00:28:23.880 --> 00:28:27.920
But a fun fact is that BJ Novak was the first person cast on the show.

718
00:28:28.560 --> 00:28:29.340
He was?

719
00:28:29.480 --> 00:28:29.900
He was.

720
00:28:30.200 --> 00:28:32.200
Greg Daniels saw him doing stand-up comedy.

721
00:28:32.520 --> 00:28:35.040
And he thought he was like, this guy.

722
00:28:35.340 --> 00:28:36.480
I want this guy.

723
00:28:36.480 --> 00:28:43.800
And so he offered BJ a role as both a writer and the role of Ryan the Temp on the show.

724
00:28:43.980 --> 00:28:47.980
So when I came in for my audition, BJ was already caSt.

725
00:28:48.180 --> 00:28:49.400
He was the very first person.

726
00:28:49.400 --> 00:28:56.140
And he was also the first example of that synergy of having people who were both performers and writers for the show.

727
00:28:56.240 --> 00:28:57.440
Which was so great.

728
00:28:57.540 --> 00:29:01.760
Something that we haven't talked about, but we will talk about it many times as the show goes,

729
00:29:01.760 --> 00:29:06.420
is that what a wonderful creative collaboration we had between the writers and the actors.

730
00:29:06.660 --> 00:29:08.100
And that was because of Greg Daniels.

731
00:29:08.180 --> 00:29:11.520
He sort of set the tone that he wanted this creative collaboration.

732
00:29:11.520 --> 00:29:12.980
He wanted to hear our ideas.

733
00:29:13.160 --> 00:29:14.760
And he wanted writers on the set.

734
00:29:14.920 --> 00:29:18.000
And I think we were just so much the better for it.

735
00:29:18.140 --> 00:29:21.680
I've been on other shows now where the writers and the actors are separate.

736
00:29:21.680 --> 00:29:22.840
And there's not a lot of collaboration.

737
00:29:23.420 --> 00:29:26.900
It feels weird after having experienced something else.

738
00:29:27.200 --> 00:29:30.620
No, Greg really trusted us to be the experts of our characters.

739
00:29:30.620 --> 00:29:35.380
So whenever he was trying to mull something over or justify something,

740
00:29:35.740 --> 00:29:37.480
he would come to us and ask us.

741
00:29:37.580 --> 00:29:39.800
Just in the way that he went to Steve and said,

742
00:29:40.120 --> 00:29:43.260
hey, do you have any thoughts on who Michael Scott's heroes would be?

743
00:29:43.420 --> 00:29:43.620
Yeah.

744
00:29:43.680 --> 00:29:48.080
He trusted us to have done our homework and know our characters so well that we could contribute.

745
00:29:48.400 --> 00:29:49.160
Yeah, it was great.

746
00:29:49.240 --> 00:29:49.960
It was really special.

747
00:29:50.760 --> 00:29:54.700
So also in that scene, that is the famous stapler in Jell-O.

748
00:29:55.080 --> 00:29:55.340
Yeah.

749
00:29:55.680 --> 00:29:56.680
The stapler goes in Jell-O.

750
00:29:56.920 --> 00:29:59.240
I have seen so many people recreate this.

751
00:29:59.700 --> 00:30:00.140
How?

752
00:30:00.140 --> 00:30:00.600
How?

753
00:30:00.600 --> 00:30:01.700
I am blown away.

754
00:30:01.880 --> 00:30:06.460
Every time I see a fan tag me in a photo where they put something in Jell-O, I'm like,

755
00:30:06.620 --> 00:30:07.380
I'm very impressed.

756
00:30:07.460 --> 00:30:08.220
Where to go?

757
00:30:08.420 --> 00:30:08.780
I know.

758
00:30:09.060 --> 00:30:12.980
Something we haven't mentioned is the flirtation between Jim and Pam in this episode.

